{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba memo.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Staggered-adoption DiD adjudication\n     2\t\n     3\tFor these counties, the standard warning about staggered TWFE is relevant in\n     4\tprinciple but does not materially change the substantive result. The naive\n     5\tcounty- and year-fixed-effects estimate is -0.0365 (SE 0.0133) log points.\n     6\tAll three heterogeneity-robust alternatives are close: Callaway--Sant'Anna\n     7\t(C&S) is -0.0398 (SE 0.0129) with not-yet-treated controls and -0.0400 (SE\n     8\t0.0122) with never-treated controls; the Roth--Sant'Anna efficient estimator\n     9\tis -0.0471 (SE 0.0116). Thus, changing the estimator does not reverse the\n    10\tsign, remove the apparent effect, or produce a substantively different\n    11\tballpark. The data indicate an employment decline of roughly 4 percent, not a\n    12\tfragile conclusion that exists only in TWFE.\n    13\t\n    14\tThe Goodman--Bacon decomposition helps explain that agreement. Fully 86.28%\n    15\tof the naive TWFE identifying weight is on treated-versus-never-treated 2x2\n    16\tcomparisons. Only 5.39% is on later-versus-earlier-treated comparisons--the\n    17\tcomparison in which the earlier cohort is already treated when used as a\n    18\tcontrol. The remaining 8.33% is earlier-versus-later-treated comparisons.\n    19\tThe Bacon component weights shown by the package are non-negative; the 5.39%\n    20\tfigure is exposure to the comparison type that can induce problematic implicit\n    21\tweights when effects are dynamic, rather than a literal share of negative\n    22\tnumbers in the reported Bacon table. It is small here, and the robust estimates\n    23\tconfirm that it is not moving the pooled conclusion much.\n    24\t\n    25\tThe dynamic C&S event study using not-yet-treated controls is also broadly\n    26\tconsistent with parallel trends. With event time -1 as the reference, the two\n    27\testimated leads are 0.027 (95% CI -0.007 to 0.061) at -3 and 0.024 (95% CI\n    28\t-0.004 to 0.053) at -2. The group-time pretrend Wald test has p = 0.168.\n    29\tNeither is decisive evidence *for* parallel trends--the first lead is somewhat\n    30\timprecise--but there is no conventional statistically detectable pre-treatment\n    31\tdeparture. Post-treatment effects turn negative and are clearly negative from\n    32\tevent time +1 onward.\n    33\t\n    34\tI would report the C&S simple ATT with not-yet-treated controls: -0.0398 log\n    35\tpoints (SE 0.0129), or about a 4% reduction in teen employment. It uses valid\n    36\tnot-yet-treated comparison units without treating already-treated counties as\n    37\tcontrols, and it agrees almost exactly with the never-treated version. This is\n    38\tnot a claim that plain TWFE is generally safe under staggered adoption. Rather,\n    39\tin this particular short panel the problematic comparison has little weight,\n    40\tand direct robust re-estimation shows that the substantive inference survives\n    41\tit. The causal reading still rests on parallel trends and no anticipation,\n    42\twhich these data can support only indirectly.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
